// Grow lights light the plots near them (2026-09-26, gardening depth).
// Read by src/systems/farming/lighting.rs, which carries the model; this file
// carries the numbers and where each came from. Sources read on 2026-09-26.
//
// THE MODEL. A powered grow light puts out PPF = watts x ppe_umol_per_j
// micromoles of photons a second. After dark it gives a plot a second day of
// growth only if it delivers that plot the crop's daily light integral (DLI)
// through the night, so the photon flux density it must hold on each square
// metre is target_dli / the seconds of night (24 h x (1 - the daylight share,
// farming::DAYLIGHT_FRACTION, 0.5) = 43,200 s). The area one light covers is
// PPF / that density; it covers the plots nearest it first, within reach_m,
// and a plot it only partly covers grows that share of a night's growth. Two
// lights over one bed add up. Outdoor fields get only the sun.
//
// A 100 W light: 100 x 2.30 = 230 umol/s over 17e6 / 43,200 = 394 umol/m2/s
// covers 0.58 m2. Run through the 12 h night that is 1.2 kWh for 0.58 m2,
// 2.1 kWh per m2 a day, which agrees with the 2.2 kWh per m2 a day for greens
// in docs/design/self-sufficiency.md, worked out independently.
//
// SOURCES
//
// ppe_umol_per_j: DesignLights Consortium, "Technical Requirements for
//   LED-based Horticultural Lighting, Version 3.0" (2024 PDF,
//   https://designlights.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/DLC_HORT-Technical-Requirements-V3-0_FINAL_05132024.pdf,
//   summarised at https://designlights.org/our-work/horticultural-lighting/technical-requirements/hort-v3-0/):
//   "The Hort V3.0 PPE threshold has been increased to a minimum of
//   2.30 umol x J-1". The minimum a listed LED grow light may have, so a
//   plain listed fixture; better ones reach about 3.
//
// target_dli_mol_m2_d: Cornell University CEA Program, "Hydroponic Lettuce
//   Handbook" (2013), Lighting section:
//   https://cpb-us-e1.wpmucdn.com/blogs.cornell.edu/dist/8/8824/files/2019/06/Cornell-CEA-Lettuce-Handbook-.pdf
//   "It should be noted that 17 mol/m2/d is the light integral that worked
//   best for the particular cultivar of boston bibb lettuce that we used."
//   One figure for every crop until each species carries its own (the next
//   rung): fruiting crops such as tomato want more, and some lettuces tip-burn
//   above about 15 (the same page).
//
// cup_canopy_m2: the same handbook, pond growing area: "After re-spacing (from
//   97 plants m-2 to 38 plants m-2)". 1 / 38 = 0.026 m2 of canopy a finished
//   plant needs, used for each planted cup of a tower.
//
// reach_m: GAME ESTIMATE. A fixture hangs over the bed it serves; light
//   falls off with the square of distance, so a bed a few metres away gets a
//   negligible share. Plots whose centre is farther than this, measured
//   across the floor, get none of the light.
(
    ppe_umol_per_j: 2.30,
    target_dli_mol_m2_d: 17.0,
    cup_canopy_m2: 0.026,
    reach_m: 3.0,
)
